    <h1>How Commercial Incense Manufacturing Is Structured</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      This page explains, in neutral terms, how commercial Chinese botanical incense is
      manufactured and quality-controlled. It is a knowledge explainer, not a sales page —
      there is no quote process, no catalog, and nothing to buy here.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Commercial incense is made from botanical materials — woods,
      resins, herbs, and flowers — formed into sticks (线香), cones (塔香), coils (盘香), and sachets
      (香囊), or supplied as raw material (香材). The craft runs through a fixed sequence: sorting,
      grinding, sieving, blending, forming, drying, and finishing — followed by quality control.
    </div>

    <h2>The product formats</h2>
    <p>
      The same botanical materials reach the market in several physical forms, each with a different
      burn behavior and use:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Incense sticks</strong> (线香) — the common modern format.</li>
      <li><strong>Incense cones</strong> (塔香) and <strong>coils</strong> (盘香).</li>
      <li><strong>Sachets</strong> (香囊) — scented pouches, worn or placed rather than burned.</li>
      <li><strong>Raw materials</strong> (香材) — woods, resins, herbs, and flowers, supplied whole, chipped, or powdered.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These formats sit on top of a documented ingredient reference of 150 botanical entities,
      organized by material family, on the
      <a href="/ingredients/">ingredient encyclopedia</a>. This page adds no material claims beyond
      what is already published there.
    </p>

    <h2>Materials and the authenticity question</h2>
    <p>
      Incense begins with the material, and the useful question is <em>what</em> the material is —
      species, part, and grade — not what it is called. The discipline is the same one documented in
      the <a href="/blog/incense-authentication-database/">authentication database</a>: match the
      material form to the downstream process (whole, chip, powder, resin), and verify species and
      grade rather than trusting a trade name.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because botanicals are natural materials, not standardized chemicals, the same name can cover
      real variation. A "sandalwood" may be <em>Santalum album</em>, another <em>Santalum</em>
      species, or a synthetic fragrance; a "borneol" may be a natural crystal, a leaf-distilled
      extract, or a synthetic isomer mixture. The
      <a href="/blog/incense-substitution-cross-reference/">substitution cross-reference</a> maps
      these label traps.
    </p>

